The statement is **false**.

**Counterexample.** Take $X = \mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, which is a locally compact Hausdorff space. Let
$$K = \{0\} \cup [1,2].$$
This set is compact: it is closed and bounded in $\mathbb{R}$ (equivalently, it is a finite union of compact sets, the singleton $\{0\}$ and the closed interval $[1,2]$). Take $x = 0$, so $x \in K$.

Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists an open set $U$ in $\mathbb{R}$ with $0 \in U \subseteq K$. Since $U$ is open and contains $0$, there exists $\varepsilon > 0$ such that $(-\varepsilon, \varepsilon) \subseteq U$. Choose any point $t$ with $0 < t < \min(\varepsilon, 1)$, for instance $t = \min(\varepsilon, 1)/2$. Then $t \in (-\varepsilon, \varepsilon) \subseteq U \subseteq K$. But $0 < t < 1$, so $t \notin \{0\}$ and $t \notin [1,2]$, hence $t \notin K$ — a contradiction.

Therefore no such open set $U$ exists, even though $X = \mathbb{R}$ is locally compact Hausdorff, $K$ is compact, and $x \in K$.

**Justification.** The flaw in the statement is that it confuses "every point has *some* compact neighborhood" (the definition of local compactness) with "every compact set containing a point is a neighborhood of that point." A compact set $K$ containing $x$ need not contain any open neighborhood of $x$; the point $x$ may lie on the boundary of $K$ or in an isolated part of $K$ with no interior relative to $X$. In the counterexample, $0$ is an isolated point of $K$ but has no open neighborhood in $\mathbb{R}$ contained in $K$, since every open interval around $0$ spills outside $\{0\}\cup[1,2]$.

